module Module
  # @module_doc
  #
  # Module — introspecting and calling into modules by value.
  #
  # A module is itself a value in Ramos: `Kernel` (or `Integer`, or any module
  # written) can be bound, passed around, and stored — `is_module` tests for
  # one and `type_of` names it. This module is what you do with one once you
  # have it, when the function to call is not known at the call site.
  #
  #   # ramos doctest setup
  #   module Greeter
  #     fn hello(name)
  #       "hi #{name}"
  #
  #     fnp secret()
  #       42
  #
  #   Module.functions(Greeter)                    # == ["hello"]
  #   Module.apply(Greeter, "hello", ["Andrew"])    # == "hi Andrew"
  #
  # Conventions:
  #   - both functions only ever see a module's *public* functions — `fnp`
  #     stays exactly as unreachable from here as it is from a written
  #     `Greeter.secret()` call outside the module.
  #   - a function is named with a `String` (`"hello"`), not a `Symbol` —
  #     matching `type_of`, since a module's own name is a `String` too.

  fn functions(m)
    # @doc
    #
    # The names of the public functions `m` defines, in declaration order.
    # `fnp` functions are never included — they are not callable from outside
    # `m` either.
    #
    # @param m: the module to inspect
    # @return a `List` of `String` function names, in declaration order
    #
    #   Module.functions(Greeter)   # == ["hello"]
    native("module_functions", [m])

  fn apply(m, fn_name, args)
    # @doc
    #
    # Resolves `fn_name` on module `m` and calls it with `args`, a list of its
    # arguments — like `Kernel.apply`, but the function is looked up by name on
    # a module rather than held as a lambda. Use it when *which* function to
    # call is only known at runtime (a dispatch table, a plugin name read from
    # config); otherwise call the function directly.
    #
    # Has no Ramos body: the interpreter intercepts the call, because
    # resolving `fn_name` and running it needs the evaluator, which a native
    # cannot reach. Raises if `m` has no public function named `fn_name`.
    #
    # @param m: the module to call a function on
    # @param fn_name: the function's name, as a `String`
    # @param args: a `List` of the arguments to pass
    # @return whatever the resolved function returns
    #
    #   Module.apply(Integer, "abs", [-7])   # == 7
    module_apply(m, fn_name, args)
